---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import CtaBand from "../components/CtaBand.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Enforcement & takedowns"
  description="How a Kirpik Chain of Proof certificate supports a DMCA notice, a hosting complaint and Google deindexing when your Shopify text is stolen. Practical, honest, and not legal advice."
>
  <PageHero
    title="From proof to takedown."
    lead="Detecting your copy on another store is the easy part. This page covers what happens next: the certificate, the DMCA notice, the host complaint and the search deindexing request."
  />

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container enf">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Takedowns run on evidence, not indignation</h2>
        <p>
          Hosts, registrars and search engines process complaints at industrial
          scale. The person reading yours has never heard of your store, has no
          way to know who wrote what, and sees dozens of claims a day that turn
          out to be competitors squabbling. What moves them is a specific,
          checkable demonstration that the text is yours and predates the copy.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is a harder bar for text than for images. Two product
          descriptions can legitimately resemble each other, and "we sell the
          same thing" is a common defence. The job of the evidence is to close
          that door: not "these pages are similar" but "this exact text carries
          this store's watermark, was protected on this date, and appeared on
          the infringing page later".
        </p>

        <h2>What a Chain of Proof certificate contains</h2>
        <p>
          When Kirpik detects your watermark in a suspect page, it can generate
          a Chain of Proof certificate: a dated ownership document built to be
          read by an abuse desk. Each certificate records:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>A unique certificate ID and the scope of the document</li>
          <li>Your date of protection and the Protection Signature, a SHA-256 fingerprint of your original content</li>
          <li>The content title and the original URL on your store</li>
          <li>The infringing URL, its own Last-Modified headers and the detection date</li>
          <li>The Detected Signature of the copy, the count of recovered Watermark Security Markers and a confidence figure</li>
          <li>The earliest Internet Archive snapshot of the infringing page, corroborating the timeline through a source neither side controls</li>
          <li>A side-by-side comparison of your original and the modified copy</li>
          <li>A QR code linking to a public verification page, so anyone can confirm the certificate is genuine without trusting the PDF</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The point of all that detail is simple: the person handling your
          complaint should be able to verify your claim in minutes, from their
          desk, without taking your word for anything.
        </p>

        <h2>Route one: the DMCA notice</h2>
        <p>
          For most stolen text the first move is a DMCA takedown notice sent to
          the infringing site's host. A valid notice identifies your original
          work, identifies the infringing material by URL, and includes the
          required statements and your signature. Attach or link the Chain of
          Proof certificate as your supporting evidence. Text is fully
          protected by copyright the moment you write it, so product
          descriptions, buying guides and blog articles all qualify.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our guide <a href="/learn/dmca-for-stolen-text/">The DMCA process for
          stolen text, step by step</a> walks through finding the right
          recipient, wording each required element, and what to do when a
          counter-notice arrives.
        </p>

        <h2>Route two: the hosting complaint</h2>
        <p>
          Some sites sit with hosts that operate their own abuse processes
          alongside or instead of the DMCA, especially outside the United
          States. The approach is the same: identify the host through a WHOIS
          or DNS lookup, find their abuse contact, and submit the infringing
          URL together with your certificate. Hosts vary widely in speed, but a
          verifiable evidence package consistently outperforms an angry email.
        </p>

        <h2>Route three: deindexing from Google</h2>
        <p>
          Sometimes the practical damage is not that the copy exists but that
          it ranks. Google accepts copyright removal requests for search
          results, and a successful request removes the infringing page from
          results even while the page itself stays up. For a store whose stolen
          copy is outranking the original, this is often the fastest way to
          stop the bleeding. See
          <a href="/learn/deindexing-stolen-copy/">Getting stolen copy removed
          from Google's index</a> for the process.
        </p>

        <h2>When to bring in a specialist</h2>
        <p>
          Most cases end after one well-evidenced notice. A minority do not:
          the infringer hides behind privacy services, reposts under a new
          domain, or ignores everything. For those, we recommend engaging an
          intellectual property solicitor or a takedown specialist rather than
          grinding through it alone. Arrive with your Chain of Proof
          certificate and the paper trail of notices you have already sent, and
          you have done most of their discovery work for them, which tends to
          show up in the bill.
        </p>

        <h2>A note on what this page is</h2>
        <p>
          This page is general information about how takedown processes work,
          written by a software company, not a law firm. It is not legal
          advice, and enforcement rules differ between countries. For anything
          contested or high-stakes, talk to a qualified professional in your
          jurisdiction.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="enf__side">
        <div class="card sidecard sidecard--steps">
          <h3>The playbook</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Detect your watermark in the suspect page</li>
            <li>Generate the Chain of Proof certificate</li>
            <li>Send the DMCA notice or host complaint</li>
            <li>Request Google deindexing if the copy ranks</li>
            <li>Bring in a specialist for the stubborn few</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="card sidecard">
          <span class="tag tag--orange">Evidence first</span>
          <h3>The certificate only exists if you protected first</h3>
          <p>Kirpik can only certify text that carried your watermark before it was copied. Protection is the step you cannot do retroactively.</p>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost sidecard__btn" href="/how-it-works/">How protection works</a>
        </div>
        <div class="card sidecard sidecard--muted">
          <p>General information, not legal advice. For contested cases, consult a qualified professional in your jurisdiction.</p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    heading="Put the evidence in place before you need it."
    sub="Watermark your descriptions, articles and pages now, so the day a copy appears you are generating a certificate instead of gathering screenshots."
  />
</Base>

<style>
  .enf { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.7fr 1fr; gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem); align-items: start; }
  .enf__side { display: grid; gap: 1.1rem; position: sticky; top: 96px; }
  .sidecard { padding: 1.5rem; }
  .sidecard h3 { margin: 0.6rem 0 0.5rem; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .sidecard p { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .sidecard__btn { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.92rem; padding: 0.75em 1.1em; }
  .sidecard--steps ol { margin: 0.4rem 0 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; display: grid; gap: 0.55rem; }
  .sidecard--steps li { color: var(--ink-soft); padding-left: 0.2rem; }
  .sidecard--steps li::marker { color: var(--orange); font-weight: 600; font-family: var(--font-mono); }
  .sidecard--muted { background: var(--paper-2); box-shadow: none; }
  .sidecard--muted p { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-faint); }
  @media (max-width: 880px) { .enf { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .enf__side { position: static; } }
</style>
